  {"id":"airway-anatomy-2","topic":"Airway Anatomy","domain":"Anesthesia: basic & clinical sciences (relevant anatomy, respiratory/cardiac/renal/neuro physiology, pharmacokinetics/dynamics principles, physics of gases & flow, statistics)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"What nerve provides motor innervation to all intrinsic laryngeal muscles EXCEPT the cricothyroid?","answer":"The recurrent laryngeal nerve (RLN); the cricothyroid is innervated by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve.","rationale":"RLN injury (e.g. during thyroid surgery) causes vocal cord paralysis; the SLN external branch provides the only motor supply to the cricothyroid.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Stanford CA-1","page":72}],"confusable_with":"Superior laryngeal nerve (sensory above cords + motor to cricothyroid only)"},
  {"id":"airway-anatomy-3","topic":"Airway Anatomy","domain":"Anesthesia: basic & clinical sciences (relevant anatomy, respiratory/cardiac/renal/neuro physiology, pharmacokinetics/dynamics principles, physics of gases & flow, statistics)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"What does the internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve innervate?","answer":"Sensory innervation to the larynx above the glottis (vocal cords) up to the epiglottis; afferent input between epiglottis and vocal cords.","rationale":"Blocking the internal SLN (via greater cornu of hyoid approach) obtunds the laryngeal cough reflex for awake intubation.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Stanford CA-1","page":72}],"confusable_with":"RLN (below the cords, sensation + all motor except cricothyroid)"},
